# Changelog

All notable changes to this project will be documented in this file.

The format is based on [Keep a Changelog](https://keepachangelog.com/en/1.0.0/),
and this project adheres to [Semantic Versioning](https://semver.org/spec/v2.0.0.html).

## 4.6.10 - 2026-08-24
### Fixed
- **Every Celery control-command reply (`inspect active`/`reserved`/`stats`, `revoke`, worker-startup `mingle`, ...) has always silently failed to reply**, on every tenant, since `sportfac/celery.py` was first written - `app.conf.broker_transport_options` carried a raw `kombu.Queue` instance (`"queue_options": {"bindings": [Queue(key_prefix + "_default")]}`), and every reply attempt tries to JSON-encode a slice of `app.conf` on its way back, crashing with `EncodeError("Object of type Queue is not JSON serializable")` from the worker's side - invisible to the caller, who just sees `celery inspect` time out with "No nodes replied within time constraint" no matter how long the timeout, since the worker had already fully handled the request and only choked on sending the reply. Confirmed live in Coppet's `worker.log` (2026-08-24) after `celery inspect` kept failing across every tenant despite `supervisorctl status` showing every worker healthy and the environment-variable/timeout angles already ruled out. `queue_options`/`bindings` isn't an option kombu's Redis transport actually reads (that's AMQP/RabbitMQ-transport doc territory) - removed, it never did anything functional. `key_prefix` (the real, documented Redis-transport option behind per-tenant isolation) is untouched. Reproduced the exact crash locally (`json.dumps({'bindings': [Queue(...)]})`) and confirmed the fixed config serializes cleanly before and after

## 4.6.9 - 2026-08-23
### Added
- Registration wizard's payment step (`wizard/payment.html`) now shows the Swiss QR-invoice payment slip (when `payment__DISPLAY_QR_INVOICE` is on and the bank details are valid), matching what the parent's own bill page and the backend already display - this page had its own hand-rolled copy of the IBAN/amount block predating that addition and was simply never updated when it landed there. Also added a "Download PDF" button (reusing the existing polling widget from `pdf-download-button.html`, which now supports an `inline=True` mode to sit next to another button instead of in its own paragraph) and a note that the invoice will arrive by email within the hour - both next to the existing "Register new activities" button. The button only appears once `bill.pdf` already exists: this page is reached by every family at once right after a registration rush, and offering a button that itself triggers generation there is exactly the burst worth avoiding - the confirmation email generates it shortly after anyway, and the button's only job here is to hand over what's already sitting there. Reported by Rémo (Kepchup)
- Registration wizard: when `KEPCHUP_ACTIVITIES_CAN_REGISTER_SAME_ACTIVITY_TWICE` is off, a course's detail popup now shows "Une seule inscription par activité par enfant pour l'année scolaire" right under "Places disponibles" - so a parent sees the constraint upfront on their *first* registration for that activity, rather than only discovering it if they later try a second course and find it greyed out. Verified the existing block itself is already correct: a course whose activity the child is already registered to renders as `unavailable` (grey, not just visually but genuinely non-clickable - `Course.toEvents` sets `clickable: false` for that class and `eventClick` bails out before ever opening the modal), so the popup was never reachable for that case to begin with - confirmed live, clicking a greyed-out course only shows its tooltip, no modal. Reported by Rémo (Kepchup)

### Fixed
- `generate_invoice_pdf` (the task the "Download PDF" button's polling triggers on first click) had no `rate_limit`, unlike `send_bill_pdf_email`'s identical Playwright-driven `bill.generate_pdf()` call, which is deliberately throttled to `12/m` because it shares a single-concurrency Celery worker with the web server itself. Without a matching limit, a burst of download clicks (exactly what the new wizard button invites, all at once right after a registration rush) would queue ahead of that throttled email task unthrottled, delaying confirmation emails for everyone - not just whoever clicked. Now `rate_limit="12/m"` too
- `pdf-download-button.html`'s label/loading text spans were classed `label`/`loading` - `label` collides with Bootstrap's own `.label` (small pill/badge component), which forces `font-size: 11.844px` on it, shrinking it well below the button's actual `btn-large` size and only affecting that one span - so the button visibly changed size the moment it swapped to "Generating PDF…" (unaffected, normal size) and back. Renamed to `pdf-btn-label`/`pdf-btn-loading`. Predates this release - the same bug was already live wherever this button was already used (the parent's and backend's own bill detail pages), not just the new wizard usage above. Reported by Greg while reviewing the wizard payment page changes
- Same cache-busting gap as 4.6.6's `style.css` fix, found again while adding the popup text above: `static/partials/activity-detail.html` (fetched by `$modal`) and `static/partials/activity-list.html` (fetched by `$routeProvider`, previously behind a hand-maintained `?v=3` counter) had no reliable versioning, so a browser that already had either cached could keep serving the stale one across a deploy. Both now use a new `window.KEPCHUP_VERSION` (set from `{{ VERSION }}` in `activities_app.html`) - see `TECH_DEBT.md` for why this is now the third separate hand-rolled cache-busting mechanism in the codebase

## 4.6.8 - 2026-08-23
### Fixed
- **Regression from 4.6.5, live in production**: any logged-in, non-staff visitor's request (even a plain `favicon.ico` carrying their session cookie - seen on Coppet) crashed with `relation "activities_coursesinstructors" does not exist`. 4.6.5's `VersionMiddleware.hostname_from_request` (`sportfac/middleware.py`) checked `request.user.is_kepchup_staff` to decide whether to honor a session's non-production pin - but `is_kepchup_staff`'s `is_instructor` check queries `activities.CoursesInstructors`, a tenant-scoped table, and this method runs *before* the tenant schema is switched (it's what decides which schema to switch to) - so it hit whatever schema the DB connection was last left on (`public` on a fresh one), where no `activities_*` table exists at all. Now checks only `is_manager`/`is_restricted_manager`/`is_superuser` directly - plain fields on `FamilyUser`, safe regardless of current schema since `profiles` is a `SHARED_APPS` model. Trade-off: an instructor-only account (no other role) no longer stays pinned to a preview period through this middleware; `ChangeYearFormView` itself (where the pin is set) is unaffected, since normal views already run with the schema resolved. Added a regression test (`backend/tests/test_views/test_middleware.py`) that reproduces the crash by explicitly forcing the DB connection to the `public` schema before calling `hostname_from_request`, matching the real precondition - the bug does not reproduce on whatever schema the test harness happens to default to

## 4.6.7 - 2026-08-23
### Fixed
- `Course.has_waiting_list` (denormalized, gates `accepts_registrations`) could get permanently stuck `True` even with an empty waiting list and room available, silently blocking new registrations - it was only ever updated by a signal on `WaitingSlot` `post_save`/`post_delete` (`activities/signals.py`), with nothing to correct it if it ever drifted once (race condition, a state predating that signal, a direct DB edit...). `nb_participants` already self-heals on every `Course.save()` (recomputed from `self.participants.count()`); `has_waiting_list` now does the same, recomputed from `self.waiting_slots.exists()`. Reported for Oron's BOXE.2 (`has_waiting_list=True`, 0 participants, 0 actual waiting slots). `scripts/fix_stale_waiting_list_flags.py` added to find and correct any course already stuck this way, across every tenant

## 4.6.6 - 2026-08-23
### Fixed
- `style.css`/`not_production.css` had no cache-busting: `{% static %}` resolves to a fixed URL (`STATICFILES_STORAGE` is plain `StaticFilesStorage`, no content hashing), and unlike `app.min.js` (already `?v={{ VERSION }}`) these two links never got a version query string, so a browser's ordinary cache kept serving the CSS from *before* a deploy until a hard reload bypassed it. Reported after deploying 4.6.5 to Oron: the new sibling hatched-green style didn't apply on a normal reload - FullCalendar's own default (pale blue) rendered instead, since the `.fc-event.sibling` rule simply didn't exist yet in the cached stylesheet. `montreux_epa`/`montreux_passvac` already carried this exact fix for `style.css` from an earlier incident, but it was never applied anywhere else. Added `?v={{ VERSION }}` to both links in the default `templates/base.html` and in all 14 theme overrides (`themes/*/templates/base.html`) - each theme keeps its own copy of this file. Per-theme CSS (`coppet.css`, `oron.css`, etc.) has the same unversioned-URL issue but wasn't touched here; worth a separate pass since it changes far less often

## 4.6.5 - 2026-08-23
### Added
- Backend > Manage periods: a "Log everyone out" button, independent of switching periods, to force everyone but the clicking admin to reload and get resolved onto the current production tenant right away - for catching up on visitors whose sessions went stale before the self-healing fix below existed, without waiting for their session to expire on its own

### Fixed
- **Security/data-integrity**: a visitor's session could stay pinned to a since-retired, non-production tenant indefinitely, showing them the staff-only "not production" banner and letting them browse/register into a closed period instead of the current one - with no admin action involved on their part. `VersionMiddleware.hostname_from_request` (`sportfac/middleware.py`) trusted whatever tenant was cached in `request.session` forever, including for anonymous/regular visitors: on their first-ever visit it correctly pinned them to whichever tenant was production *at that time*, but never re-checked that pin against reality afterwards. Once the real production tenant changed (a new school year opening), anyone whose session predated the switch (e.g. returning from a bookmark or a stale Google result) silently kept seeing the old, closed tenant. The safety net meant to prevent this - `log_everyone_out`, called on every production switch to force a fresh session for everyone - has been silently doing nothing in production: it deletes from the DB-backed `django.contrib.sessions.models.Session` model, while production's `SESSION_ENGINE` is cache-backed (Redis), so no real session was ever actually cleared. Only `kepchup_staff` explicitly previewing another period may now stay pinned to a non-production tenant; every other visitor is always resolved to the live production tenant on each request, self-healing any stale pin regardless of whether `log_everyone_out` runs. Reported 2026-08-22: an external parent found a non-production, red-bannered 2025-2026 page via a Google result. `log_everyone_out` (`backend/tasks.py`) now also actually clears production's cache-backed session store (it now checks `SESSION_ENGINE` and clears matching keys in the `SESSION_CACHE_ALIAS` cache instead of only deleting from the DB-backed `Session` model), still sparing the `exceptions` session keys (e.g. the admin who triggered the switch) rather than logging everyone including them out - belt-and-suspenders alongside the middleware self-heal above, not required for it to work
- Registration wizard's weekly calendar: no course ever appeared on a Sunday. `Course.getStartDate()`/`getEndDate()` (`assets/js/activities/services.js`) project each course's fixed weekday onto the current real-world week using `today.getDate() - today.getDay() + 1`, which only lands on this week's Monday for `getDay()` 1-6 (Mon-Sat) - on a Sunday (`getDay() === 0`) it lands on *next* Monday instead, so every course rendered one week ahead of the calendar's visible current-week view and the grid looked empty. `controllers.js` already special-cased this same Monday/Sunday edge case for the calendar's own `initialDate`; `services.js` now uses the same corrected formula

### Changed
- Registration wizard's weekly calendar: a course another sibling is registered to (the "taken by another family member" hint) now always renders, unconditionally, whenever that registration exists - it's purely informational (helps a parent spot that two children would need to be in two different places at the same time) and never blocks or hides the currently-viewed child's own available/registered event on that same course, which renders independently alongside it. It also now has its own distinct style - white with a pale green hatched border (`sibling` class/`.fc-event.sibling`), color echoing the "registered" green since it does represent a registration - instead of sharing the solid grey `unavailable` look with the unrelated "already registered to this activity, can't register twice" (`KEPCHUP_ACTIVITIES_CAN_REGISTER_SAME_ACTIVITY_TWICE`) state, so the two meanings are no longer visually indistinguishable. Its hover tooltip now also names which sibling it belongs to (e.g. "Athlétisme (Romain)"), needed since the event itself carries no name

## 4.6.4 - 2026-08-23
### Fixed
- Reverted: the "taken by another family member" grey hint had been made to disappear whenever the course was still open/eligible for the viewed child - this made it inconsistent (visible for some courses a sibling was registered to, silently absent for others) and is superseded by the unconditional, visually distinct fix in 4.6.5 above

## 4.6.3 - 2026-08-22
### Fixed
- Registration wizard's weekly calendar: a course could render as unavailable (grey, non-clickable) for a child solely because it shared the exact same day/time as one of that child's *other* registrations, in a completely different activity - even though registering a child for two courses at the same time has always been allowed (a warning about the clash is shown elsewhere in the flow; the calendar itself was never meant to enforce it). This "schedule overlap" check isn't part of the actual 4-state design (available/registered/blocked-by-`KEPCHUP_ACTIVITIES_CAN_REGISTER_SAME_ACTIVITY_TWICE`/taken-by-a-sibling) that's been correct for years - a recent change (`0f5bece9`, "4.6.0 - performance enhancements") carried this pre-existing but never-actually-load-bearing overlap logic forward into the batched rewrite, and Oron hit it for real 2026-08-22 (a second child couldn't register to Echecs because of an unrelated Badminton registration at the same slot). Removed the schedule-overlap check (and the now-dead `$scope.overlap` helper) entirely from `updateAvailableEvents`
- `KEPCHUP_ACTIVITIES_CAN_REGISTER_SAME_ACTIVITY_TWICE=False` (forbid registering a child twice in the same activity) had no server-side enforcement at all - the wizard calendar greying out/disabling a second course of the same activity was the *only* thing standing in the way, and nothing stopped the same registration via a direct API call. `RegistrationSerializer.validate()` now rejects it too (ignoring the child's own canceled registrations in that activity, which don't count)

## 4.6.2 - 2026-08-22
### Added
- Two management commands to clean up the case-variant duplicate `FamilyUser` accounts described below: `lowercase_family_emails` (`--database`, `--dry-run`) normalizes existing accounts' email casing, reporting any pair that would collide instead of merging them; `merge_family_accounts` (`--database`, `--dry-run`) interactively merges such a colliding pair - suggesting whichever account logged in most recently, reassigning every relation pointing at the other one (children, bills, course-instructor assignments, managed activities, cancelation/validation records, payroll exports, appointments...) onto it, then `soft_delete()`-ing it. Run either via `tenant_command`/`all_tenants_command` to target a specific school-year tenant or sweep every one of them retroactively - each is scoped to one tenant's own business data, since a family's children/registrations live in that year's own schema

### Fixed
- Registration wizard's weekly calendar: when one child of a family was already registered to a course (even just with a not-yet-confirmed `waiting` registration), a sibling who was still eligible and the course still open couldn't register to it either - the "taken by another family member" grey indicator for that course silently swallowed the sibling's own available, clickable slot in the exact same day/time cell, since the calendar renders both as separate FullCalendar events with no way to tell them apart by click. Reported by Oron 2026-08-22 (Echecs: one child registered, the other's registration attempt went nowhere). That grey hint is only meant to flag a scheduling overlap for the parent's own logistics, never to block anything - `assets/js/activities/controllers.js`'s `updateOthersEvents` now skips it entirely when the course is still genuinely open and eligible for the child currently being registered, letting their own available slot render unobstructed. Everywhere else (different courses, real time conflicts) the hint is unchanged
- Registered-activities summary page (`registrations/summary.html`) showed every instructor's mobile phone number unconditionally, regardless of that instructor's "Phone visible for parents" setting - `phone_public` gating was added to the course/activity detail pages on 2026-06-09 but this page was missed, reported by La Tour-de-Peilz 2026-08-22 (instructor numbers are entered for internal payroll use, not meant to be shown to parents). Now respects `phone_public` the same way, falling back to the configured `INSTRUCTOR_FALLBACK_PHONE` preference when set
- Activities API (`/api/activities/`), `KEPCHUP_LIMIT_BY_AGE` tenants (e.g. La Tour-de-Peilz): a course restricting only one end of the age range (only a minimum, or only a maximum, but not both) crashed the entire request with a `TypeError` (comparing `None` to the child's birth date) instead of just filtering on its one real bound. Same latent bug existed for the school-year path (`KEPCHUP_LIMIT_BY_SCHOOL_YEAR` tenants) for a course restricting only one end of the school-year range - fixed there too, before it could crash the same way
- Login was case-sensitive on the email address, while password reset (Django's own `PasswordResetForm`) has always matched it case-insensitively - so a family who signed up with capital letters in their email, then later requested a password reset typing it in lowercase, would get the reset email but then fail to log in with the new password, since the account's email still had its original casing and login required an exact match. `FamilyUser` now lowercases its email on every save regardless of entry point (registration, admin, API, SSO sync), and every login path (`ModelBackend`, the multi-tenant `MasterUserBackend`, and the SSO client's email fallback lookup) now matches case-insensitively - plus a data migration to lowercase existing accounts (skipping, and reporting, any pair that would collide once lowercased). One follow-on issue specific to `MasterUserBackend` (Montreux's multi-tenant family - montreux/montreux_ski/montreux_epa/montreux_passvac - which all share one master identity DB) was caught and fixed before it could reach production: confirmed pre-existing case-variant duplicate accounts (e.g. two active accounts for the same person differing only by email case - almost certainly earlier victims of this very bug) would have turned a working login into a crash under a naive case-insensitive lookup, since it'd now match both rows at once - login now tries an exact match first and only falls back to case-insensitive when unambiguous. The full-object sync from the shared master record into each tenant's local copy (address, phone, roles... all meant to propagate - that's the point of sharing one identity across tenants) is unchanged

## 4.6.1 - 2026-08-19
### Fixed
- Family API: `ext_id` (Lagapeo ID) was writable through a child update despite being meant as read-only, and could collide with another child's ID, crashing the request with a 500. Now truly read-only, and any such DB conflict returns a clean 400 instead of crashing
- Tenant cache invalidation signal crashed (`AttributeError`) on any non-Redis cache backend (e.g. local/test `LocMemCache`)
- Backend bill detail page never showed the total amount / payment instructions block, unlike the family-facing one - it was gated behind a template variable that was never actually set. Both views now share the same content
- Wire-transfer bill: the QR-invoice code sat in a half-width column next to the payment details, rendering it smaller than needed - now stacked full-width below them
- Wire-transfer bill: the "indicate your transaction ID under communications/reason" instruction was a disconnected sentence after the payment details box - merged into the preceding sentence

## 4.6.0 - 2026-08-18
### Fixed
- Wizard step cache invalidation (`wizard/models.py`'s `clear_wizard_step_cache`, triggered by `WizardStep`'s `post_save`/`post_delete` signals) deleted a cache key (`"all_wizard_steps"`) that was never the one actually set - `WizardWorkflow` reads/writes `"all_wizard_steps_<schema>"` (`wizard/workflow.py`). Adding, editing, reordering, or removing a wizard step while the 1-hour cache was warm silently kept serving the stale step list to every family already in the registration flow, with no error anywhere. Low urgency in practice (only matters if wizard step config changes while registrations are live), but a real, confirmed bug - reproduced with a failing test before the fix (new step added, still invisible after the signal fired), passing after
- Added test coverage for `wizard/handlers.py` and `wizard/workflow.py` (previously 48%/22%, now 100%/100%) - these are the wizard's entire step-gating logic (`is_ready`/`is_complete`/`is_visible` per step, next/previous navigation), directly controlling whether a family can access or is blocked from a given registration step
- Backend admin's student list (`/backend/child/`) took 4.2-5.9s on every load, flat regardless of time of day or traffic - `ChildListView` had no pagination and its template called `reverse()` up to 5x per row, so the entire, ever-growing, unpaginated table was built server-side before any paging happened. Converted to the same server-side DataTables pattern already used for the user list (new `ChildDatatableSerializer` + `DashboardChildrenView`, `api:all_children`) - pagination, sorting and search now happen server-side, plus a search pane on blacklisted status. Also fixed a latent bug found while rewriting the template: the emergency-number `tel:` link referenced `registration.child.emergency_number` (undefined in this list context, so the link was always empty) instead of the row's own `child`
- Backend admin's registrations list (`/backend/registrations/`) had the same unpaginated-server-side-table problem as the student list above. Converted to server-side DataTables (`RegistrationDatatableSerializer` + `DashboardRegistrationsView`, `api:all_registrations`), scoped identically to the page it replaces - restricted managers still only see their own activities' registrations. Also fixed the "Validate all registrations" button, previously gated on a full-table `.count()` that would have silently broken once the list became a server-side shell, and a pre-existing bug in the old client-side search-panes config that targeted the status column by array position (`columns: [-2]`) - silently pointed at the wrong column whenever bib numbers were disabled, since that shifts every column index by one
- Backend registrations list: clicking the status search pane (e.g. "Annulée") always returned zero results despite the pane showing the correct count next to it - the filtering code assumed DataTables SearchPanes sends back an option's display label and reverse-mapped it to the underlying value, but the actually-loaded SearchPanes build sends the raw value instead; now accepts either. While adding a regression test built from a real captured browser request (rather than a hand-written one, which never exercises this), also found that `rest_framework_datatables` silently strips any serializer field that isn't one of the client's declared table columns - this was dropping several fields (`is_canceled`, `cancelation_date`, etc. on registrations; `url`, `school_name` on students) that live inside another column's client-side rendering instead of being their own column; fixed via `datatables_always_serialize` on both serializers
- Backend student list: typing anything into the search box 500'd the entire request, regardless of which column was actually meant to be searched - the school-year column's DataTables definition pointed straight at the underlying foreign key, and the global search box runs a text search across every searchable column at once, which the database rejects for a raw foreign key. Pointed the column at the school year's own value field instead
- Registration wizard's activity/course picker fired one HTTP request per visible course on every availability-polling tick. Combined with production running a fixed pool of concurrent request slots, several parallel requests per active family during a registration rush could exhaust that pool well before the number of simultaneously-registering families that had been observed to cause slowdowns. Added a batched course-lookup endpoint and updated the three request loops driving the weekly calendar to call it once per polling tick instead of once per course
- Course detail page for instructors/registered families (`/activities/courses/<id>/`) fetched the course from the database twice per request, with no query optimization applied on either fetch despite the view declaring one - an access-control mixin shared between the permission check and the page render silently overrode it with an unoptimized fetch each time. The permission check itself was also an N+1, fetching every course participant's child and family in Python to decide access instead of a single existence check. Query count no longer scales with how many families are registered to the course
- Every authenticated page load ran a per-tenant loop for instructors and restricted managers to determine which school-year tenants they have access to, one database round-trip per tenant this instance has ever had - unbounded over time, one more tenant every school year. Collapsed into a single query covering every tenant at once

## 4.5.6 - 2026-08-18
### Fixed
- Registration wizard, `KEPCHUP_LIMIT_BY_SCHOOL_YEAR` tenants (the default for every tenant except La Tour-de-Peilz, which is the one tenant running `KEPCHUP_LIMIT_BY_AGE` instead - e.g. Coppet, Vevey, the Montreux variants, Nyon, Rojalets...): a course with no school-year restriction still didn't show as available in the calendar even after 4.5.3's backend fix, because the AngularJS controller's own client-side eligibility check (`updateAvailableEvents`, mirroring the backend's) had the exact same unguarded `null` comparison, never patched
- Same tenants: clicking an unrestricted course to register (both school-year and birth-date modes) silently did nothing - a *second*, separate copy of this eligibility check (`Child.canRegister()` in `services.js`, used only at click-time) had the same unguarded-`null` bug and was never touched by any earlier fix in this series, so a course could show as available yet still reject every registration attempt
- The actual server-side registration submission (`RegistrationSerializer.validate()`) rejected every registration for a school-year-unrestricted course with "This course is not opened to children of school year X", regardless of the JS-side fixes above: `Course.school_years` returns `[]` (not "every year") when `schoolyear_min`/`schoolyear_max` are unset, so `child.school_year.year not in course.school_years` was `True` for every child - added `Course.has_school_year_restriction` (mirrors the existing `has_age_restriction`) and gated the check on it, matching how the birth-date branch just below it already worked
- Backend admin's own "add registration" form (`CourseSelectMixin`, used by staff, both `KEPCHUP_LIMIT_BY_SCHOOL_YEAR` on and off): the course dropdown was filtered with `schoolyear_min__lte=...`/`min_birth_date__gte=...` directly - SQL `NULL` comparisons are never true, so an unrestricted course silently never appeared as a selectable choice for *any* child, staff included
- La Tour-de-Peilz specifically (`KEPCHUP_LIMIT_BY_AGE`): `Course.save()` computed `min_birth_date`/`max_birth_date` from `age_min`/`age_max` when those were set, but never cleared them back to `None` when `age_min`/`age_max` were cleared - a course originally created with an age restriction, then edited in the admin to remove it, silently kept enforcing the old, no-longer-visible restriction forever, since every one of the checks above (all correctly null-guarded already) was reading a stale non-null date instead of the actual, current "no restriction" state. This was the actual root cause of the "SSF - Foot" course staying invisible throughout this whole fix series - none of the eligibility-check fixes above could have helped, since the bug was upstream of all of them, in the data itself
- `CourseResource.dehydrate_limitations()` (XLS course export) crashed (`KeyError`) exporting a school-year-unrestricted course under `KEPCHUP_LIMIT_BY_SCHOOL_YEAR`, indexing `settings.KEPCHUP_YEAR_NAMES[None]` unconditionally instead of checking `has_school_year_restriction`/`has_age_restriction` first (the age-mode branch didn't crash, but silently printed "None - None ans")

## 4.5.7 - 2026-08-18
### Fixed
- 4.5.6's `Course.save()` fix (clearing stale `min_birth_date`/`max_birth_date`) only stops *future* staleness - it doesn't repair courses whose derived dates are already stale in the database from before that fix existed, which still needed a fresh, unrelated save() to self-correct. Rather than rely on that, every remaining place that read `min_birth_date`/`max_birth_date` (or `schoolyear_min`/`schoolyear_max`) to decide *whether a restriction exists at all* - the API's structural-cache filtering (`_course_matches_birth_date`/`_course_matches_school_year`), its underlying queryset, and the backend admin's course dropdown - now checks the live-computed `has_age_restriction`/`has_school_year_restriction` instead (newly exposed on the API payload too, for the AngularJS calendar and `canRegister()` to check the same way); the derived dates are still used, but only for the actual range comparison once a restriction is confirmed to exist. Stale derived dates left over from before 4.5.6 are now simply inert everywhere, no manual re-save needed

## 4.5.5 - 2026-08-17
### Fixed
- A paid bill's detail page and PDF (`billing_partial.html`, included from `bill-pdf.html` and the family invoice-detail page) skipped the total-amount line entirely along with the payment instructions, so a paid invoice showed the list of registrations but never what was actually billed - the total (and, for wire transfers, the billing identifier the heading's own comment assumed was still shown here) now always renders; only the "please pay" instructions, bank-details box, QR-bill and pay buttons stay conditional on the bill still being unpaid

### Added
- Bill PDF: a paid bill now prints "Payé le JJ.MM.AAAA" just above the QR-invoice slip (which stays on the invoice regardless of paid status, kept for reference/reconciliation), so a printed/downloaded paid invoice carries an explicit paid marker even though the on-screen "Paid" ribbon (`invoice-part-heading.html`) is deliberately hidden in print
- Backend admin: "Régénérer le PDF de la facture" action on the Bill list, to force a fresh PDF (queued via Celery) instead of the manual delete-field-then-wait dance - clears the cached file and field for the selected bills before dispatching regeneration, to avoid a race where a fast-finishing task's fresh PDF gets wiped by the field-clearing step
- Invoice "Period" column's separator arrow used a custom webfont glyph (`icon-down-1`) that isn't reliably loaded in time in the PDF's headless-rendering context (`page.set_content()`, not a real navigation) - replaced with a plain Unicode arrow, same approach already used elsewhere on the same invoice (the "3P➜8P" activity-name arrow), which does render correctly

## 4.5.4 - 2026-08-17
### Fixed
- Invoice line items (wizard confirm/payment/success pages, backend/family invoice detail, bill PDF) showed a raw, unrendered Django template comment instead of - or mangling the layout in front of - the registration price: `{# ... #}` comments cannot span multiple lines in Django, so the multi-line explanatory comment added in 4.5.1's price-display fix was output as literal text instead of being stripped, on every invoice - regression from 4.5.1, introduced the same day it shipped

## 4.5.3 - 2026-08-17
### Fixed
- With `KEPCHUP_LIMIT_BY_SCHOOL_YEAR` on (e.g. La Tour-de-Peilz), a course with no school-year restriction (`schoolyear_min`/`schoolyear_max` both unset) crashed the whole activities-list request (`None <= year` raises `TypeError` in Python), hiding not just that course but everything else in the response too - such courses are now correctly treated as open to every school year, matching the equivalent birth-date check

## 4.5.2 - 2026-08-17
### Fixed
- Registration wizard's weekly calendar started the week on Sunday (`firstDay: 0` in the fullcalendar config) instead of Monday - wrong for every tenant, not just the one where it got reported

## 4.5.1 - 2026-08-17
### Fixed
- Invoice line-item price was recomputed live at render time (`registration.get_price_category()`) instead of showing the stored, already-billed price - could silently disagree with `Bill.total` (which sums the stored price) if a course's prices or the family's sibling order changed after the registration was created
- Registration wizard's activity/course picker (`Course.toEvents` availability check) treated a course with no age restriction as available to nobody, since comparing `null` to a birth date coerces to `NaN` in JavaScript; such courses now correctly show as available to everyone, matching the backend's own eligibility check
- `Registration.overlap()` crashed (`TypeError: unsupported operand type(s) for -: 'datetime.date' and 'NoneType'`) when comparing two same-day registrations where at least one course has no sessions yet (`KEPCHUP_EXPLICIT_SESSION_DATES` clears a course's dates to `None` until sessions are added) - now treated as no overlap instead

### Changed
- Jorat: enabled LAGAPEO-based child lookup/prefill (`KEPCHUP_DISPLAY_LAGAPEO`, `KEPCHUP_LOOKUP_LAGAPEO`), previously left at their off-by-default value

## 4.5.0 - 2026-08-14
### Fixed
- Overselling: two near-simultaneous registrations for a course's last spot could both succeed, since the "is it full" check read the course row without locking it; the course row is now locked (`SELECT ... FOR UPDATE`) and re-checked for fullness right before creating the registration
- Canceling a registration deleted its future absences, but the same save() also (re)scheduled the async absence-creation task regardless of status, which could silently recreate them moments later; that task is now a no-op for canceled registrations
- `Registration.delete_future_absences()` issued one `DELETE` per future session instead of a single bulk query - same anti-pattern as `create_future_absences()` before it was fixed

### Changed
- `Registration.create_future_absences()` moved off the registration request path onto an async Celery task (dispatched after commit), and switched from one `get_or_create()` per session to a single `bulk_create()`
- `Bill.set_paid()` / `Bill.close()` no longer loop through `Registration.save()` per registration - each iteration cascaded into a full `Bill.save()` (total recompute, QR-invoice regeneration), making an N-registration bill cost roughly O(N²) queries; registrations and rentals are now updated in bulk
- `Bill.update_total()` fetches its registrations once (with `extra_infos` prefetched) instead of querying them twice and hitting `extra_infos` once per registration
- Activities list (`ActivityViewSet`) is now cached per tenant instead of re-serialized on every request, with per-child/school-year filtering done in Python and cache invalidation wired to the Course/Activity/Session changes that actually affect it; course fullness stays live (not part of the cached payload)
- Wizard confirmation/payment pages: extra-info row counts and price-modifier lookups now reuse the existing prefetch instead of issuing one query per registration/extra info

### Added
- Bill confirmation email for wire-transfer (IBAN) payments is now split in two: an immediate, unthrottled confirmation, and a separate email carrying the actual invoice PDF, rate-limited to 12/minute so PDF generation (headless Chromium) can't spike the server during a registration rush
- `mailer.utils.render_email_content()`: renders an email template with the site's base context (`sportfac.context_processors`) always available, including outside an HTTP request, where it previously silently went missing (e.g. IBAN details missing from confirmation emails)

## 4.4.0 - 2026-08-07
### Changed
- Price display (public activity/course pages, backend course detail, wizard course modal) now uses a 2-column table (Externe / Indigène) with one row per sibling tier instead of a flat bullet list, making the family_local_3_levels pricing easier to read
- Course form's price fields no longer auto-fill each other from the base price when left empty; each tier must be entered explicitly

### Fixed
- Missing French translations for the two 3rd-tier price field labels added in 4.3.0 (`price_family_3rd` / `price_local_family_3rd`), which were falling back to raw English on backend forms, admin, and invoices
- "Download PDF" button on invoices crashed instead of retrying while the PDF was still being generated in the background (jQuery's `$.ajax` with `xhrFields: {responseType: 'blob'}` threw a synchronous `InvalidStateError` reading the pending JSON response); switched to `fetch()`
- Swiss QR-invoice PDF is regenerated when `qr_invoice` changes instead of serving a stale cached PDF indefinitely (e.g. after fixing the IBAN/address payment preferences, a bill's previously-downloaded PDF would never reflect the corrected QR code)
- Swiss QR-invoice on the web billing page rendered at its literal physical size (210mm) instead of scaling to its column, causing a misaligned/overflowing layout

## 4.3.0 - 2026-07-21
### Added
- 3-level sibling+local pricing mode (`KEPCHUP_PRICING_MODE = "family_local_3_levels"`): a 3rd `Course` price tier (`price_family_3rd` / `price_local_family_3rd`) applies to the 3rd and following children of the same family registered to the same activity, in addition to the existing 1st/2nd tiers; enabled for La Tour-de-Peilz
- Sibling-only pricing mode (`KEPCHUP_PRICING_MODE = "family"`): sibling discount without any local/external distinction

### Changed
- `KEPCHUP_USE_DIFFERENTIATED_PRICES` (boolean) replaced by `KEPCHUP_PRICING_MODE`, a per-tenant setting choosing between `simple`, `family`, `family_local` (former `True` behaviour) and `family_local_3_levels`; price displays on public/backend pages now show only the tiers actually configured on each course instead of a fixed set gated by a global flag

## 4.2.4 - 2026-07-20
### Fixed
- Teacher import (LAGAPEO): mandatory columns were hardcoded to "Numéro SPEV" and "Maîtrises", rejecting real LAGAPEO exports using "Années" instead of "Maîtrises" and lacking any ID column; column matching is now flexible (accepts "Maîtrises" or "Années", "Numéro SPEV" or "ID LAGAPEO"), only first name/last name/classes are mandatory, and teachers without an ID column are matched by first and last name instead of failing

### Added
- Help panel on the teacher import page listing the accepted LAGAPEO column names, matching the one already present on the student import page

## 4.2.3 - 2026-07-14
### Fixed
- Multi-day courses (multicourse): last active day of the week was missing from the planning calendar; caused by `getStartDate()` deriving the week base from `start_date`'s day-of-week instead of the `day` field, shifting all events one day early when `start_date` was set to a Sunday
- `all_dates` property for multicourses now returns every active session date (Mon–Fri for each week) instead of only dates spaced 7 days apart from `start_date`, fixing overlap detection between multicourses and other courses
- When copying activities to a new period (`copy_tenant_data`), the following denormalized course flags are now reset: `has_waiting_list`, `places_available_reminder_sent_on`, `announced_js`, `allow_new_participants`; previously `has_waiting_list=True` from the old period caused spurious waiting-list warnings even when the waiting slots table was empty

## 4.2.2 - 2026-06-18
### Added
- Direct download of instructor hours summary PDF (or ZIP for multiple instructors) from the course detail page, without sending any email; controlled by `KEPCHUP_DECOMPTE_DOWNLOAD` setting (default False, enabled for La Tour-de-Peilz)
- `DECOMPTE_ESTABLISHMENT` dynamic preference: establishment name printed on the hours summary PDF; falls back to school name if empty
- `KEPCHUP_DECOMPTE_SHOW_GROUP_NUMBER` setting: controls whether the course number is printed in the "Groupe d'élèves" field (default True, disabled for La Tour-de-Peilz)

### Fixed
- Production sessions now stored in a dedicated Redis cache (`sessions` alias) without tenant-aware key function, preventing login failures under multi-tenant setup

## 4.2.1 - 2026-06-09
### Fixed
- Email sending when user has no email
- PDF decompte heures: checkbox fields (MEP, JS, Instituteur) were not checked due to wrong export value ("On" instead of "Yes")

### Added
- INSTRUCTOR_FALLBACK_PHONE dynamic setting: a general phone number displayed to parents when an instructor's phone is not public
- `phone_public` flag on instructors: controls whether the instructor's phone number is visible to parents (default: False); bulk admin actions available to toggle visibility
- `KEPCHUP_DECOMPTE_ONLY_VALIDATED_SESSIONS` setting: when enabled, only sessions validated by an instructor (i.e. with a linked instructor) are included in the PDF hours summary; default False, enabled for La Tour-de-Peilz
- `KEPCHUP_SESSION_DATES_OPTIONAL` setting: when enabled, session dates are no longer required when creating or editing a course, allowing courses to be created with a description and contact info only; default False, enabled for La Tour-de-Peilz
- `KEPCHUP_AGE_OPTIONAL` setting: when enabled, age fields are no longer required on a course; courses without defined ages accept all ages, and age is not displayed in public templates; default False, enabled for La Tour-de-Peilz

